WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.400 --> 00:00:10.159
Robo's program says repeat two times,
forward, forward. Where will it stop?

2
00:00:10.379 --> 00:00:15.069
Work it out before you press Go.
Then compare three ways to school.

3
00:00:15.569 --> 00:00:21.049
Robo starts at the bottom, facing
up. The flower is four squares away.

4
00:00:21.269 --> 00:00:25.959
The program is short: repeat two
times, then forward, forward.

5
00:00:26.179 --> 00:00:29.384
To predict where it stops,
unroll the loop in your head.

6
00:00:29.604 --> 00:00:33.505
Do the body twice, and it unrolls
to four forwards in a row.

7
00:00:33.725 --> 00:00:39.437
Tap that square, then press Go.
Robo runs and lands on the flower.

8
00:00:39.937 --> 00:00:45.927
Loop two is longer: repeat three times,
forward, turn right, forward, turn left.

9
00:00:46.147 --> 00:00:51.163
Keep track of which way Robo faces.
A turn changes the next forward.

10
00:00:51.383 --> 00:00:55.470
One time round: forward up, turn
right, forward along, turn left.

11
00:00:55.690 --> 00:01:00.380
That is one up and one along. Each
time round climbs like stairs.

12
00:01:00.600 --> 00:01:05.523
Three times round is three up and
three along: Robo reaches the school.

13
00:01:06.023 --> 00:01:10.388
Making four pancakes, written
out long, is twenty-two steps.

14
00:01:10.608 --> 00:01:14.138
Mix the batter. Then pour,
cook, flip, cook, plate.

15
00:01:14.358 --> 00:01:18.816
Those five steps happen again for
every pancake. Four times over.

16
00:01:19.036 --> 00:01:23.076
Write them once inside repeat
four times, then serve at the end.

17
00:01:23.296 --> 00:01:29.055
Twenty-two steps become seven.
Repetition makes an algorithm concise.

18
00:01:29.275 --> 00:01:33.222
Change one step inside the
loop, and every pancake changes.

19
00:01:33.722 --> 00:01:38.041
Three algorithms, one task:
get from home to school.

20
00:01:38.261 --> 00:01:42.533
The short cut is four steps and
twelve minutes, but muddy when wet.

21
00:01:42.753 --> 00:01:47.444
The bus is six steps and eight minutes,
warm and dry, but it costs money.

22
00:01:47.664 --> 00:01:52.401
The main road is five steps and
fifteen minutes. Free, and no mud.

23
00:01:52.621 --> 00:01:57.265
Compare them on four things:
steps, time, cost and outcome.

24
00:01:57.485 --> 00:02:01.943
Comparing finds what is the same.
Contrasting finds what is different.

25
00:02:02.443 --> 00:02:06.437
There is no best algorithm on
its own. The purpose decides.

26
00:02:06.657 --> 00:02:10.186
For the fewest steps to
remember, the short cut wins.

27
00:02:10.406 --> 00:02:15.097
For getting there quickest, the
bus. For staying dry, the bus again.

28
00:02:15.317 --> 00:02:20.100
For no money and no mud, the main
road. Read the purpose every time.

29
00:02:20.600 --> 00:02:23.340
It is tempting to pick
the shorter algorithm.

30
00:02:23.560 --> 00:02:26.764
Shorter only wins if it
still does the whole job.

31
00:02:26.984 --> 00:02:30.746
An algorithm that leaves a
step out is shorter and wrong.

32
00:02:30.966 --> 00:02:35.888
So check the purpose first. Then
compare steps, time and cost.

33
00:02:36.388 --> 00:02:39.500
Unroll a loop in your head,
and predict where it ends.

34
00:02:39.720 --> 00:02:43.249
Repetition written once makes
a long algorithm concise.

35
00:02:43.469 --> 00:02:48.020
Compare and contrast the algorithms,
then choose for the purpose.
